First: clean the seedbed well
For example: Cacti, succulents and other desert plants: use a specific substrate for cacti, or make a mixture of 60% black peat, 30% perlite and 10% vermiculite.
Trees and shrubs that are very much on the edge of our climate zone (either by heat or by cold): for this type of plants I advise using high quality substrates or mixtures, such as 40% akadama with 60% black peat.
It's very important always maintain a certain degree of humidity in the substrate, so that the plants can grow well. Likewise, it is also necessary that use some organic fungicide -such as copper or sulfur- from the first day of sowing and following the manufacturer's recommendations, as this will prevent fungi from attacking the newly germinated shoots, and thus maintain an enviable state of health.
