U.S. solar power expected to quadruple by 2030 News
The U.S. solar industry had a record-setting year in 2020, according to a new report Tuesday by the Solar Energy Industries Association and Wood Mackenzie. New capacity additions in the U.S. solar industry jumped 43 percent over the previous year, as the industry installed a record 19.2 gigawatts of capacity, according to the U.S.
The industry also saw fourth quarter installations jump 32 percent year-over-year, even with a massive backlog of projects awaiting interconnection, and as utility-scale projects rushed to meet an anticipated decline in the Investment Tax Credit rate, the report said.
The solar industry has grown quickly over the past several years, even expanding while Trump administration enacted trade tariffs and lease rate hikes and criticized the technology as expensive.
SEIA President and CEO Abigail Ross Hopper told POLITICO that the trade group is hopeful the upcoming infrastructure package will focus on tax credits for the industry, as well as help build out transmission and the electrification of the transportation system. “I think there’s lots of things Congress could do there,” she said.
SEIA has had conversations with the Biden administration on infrastructure and tax credits, Hopper said, as well as on trade and policy initiatives to help domestic manufacturing in the U.S. The trade conversations have included both the White House and the United States Trade Representative.
But Hopper said she did not see the Biden DOJ filing as a signal of wavering support by the administration, particularly as some of Biden’s political appointees were not yet in place. “My assessment is that the Department of Justice in making that filing was just continuing to enact the legal strategy it had enacted,” adding that she did not see it as a “death knell to us.”
Get rid of the tariffs. That’s all we care about.’ We say, ‘OK, let’s talk about how we have a sustainable, healthy solar supply chain,’” Hopper said.
“I think they’re taking a look at the whole panoply of tariffs that our former president imposed, so the 201 tariffs that are solar-specific obviously being one of them, but the Section 232 steel tariffs and the Section 301 tariffs from China,” she said. “So, my understanding is that there’s a holistic evaluation of all of these tariffs happening.”
