Wind and solar power help increase the use of renewable energy in the U.S. News
According to new data released by the U.S. Energy Information Administration (EIA), driven by the continuous growth of wind power and solar energy, the use of renewable energy in the United States reached a record high in the first half of 2021. However, fossil fuels are still the country’s main energy source.
According to EIA data, the energy produced by fossil fuels has declined slightly, but it still accounts for 79% of the U.S. usage, including data as of the end of June. In the first half of 2021, fossil fuel consumption increased by 6.5% compared with the same period in 2020, of which coal consumption increased by nearly 30%.
Although the use of fossil fuels is still high, EIA predicted earlier in 2021 that by 2050, renewable energy will increase U.S. power generation by as much as 50%, and this growth will be stimulated by solar power generation . According to the EIA report, renewable energy accounts for 13% of the energy produced in the United States.
Biomass energy closely follows wind energy, accounting for 21% of US renewable energy production. Hydro power (nearly 20%), biofuels (17%) and solar energy (12%) also provide important renewable energy.
